 ],
 "notes": "Two houses and a shared pool along the edge of a slope falling to the west. From the west views (long bars of dark site stone, flush concrete roof, horizontal strip and square windows, the whole volume standing on a concrete floor slab that runs out past the stone with the supports recessed into shadow over the hillside), the corner and dusk views (a big roof slab thrown out over a glazed living room at the end of each house, a stone-parapet balcony cantilevered on the downhill corner, steps down to the ground), the gallery views (roof overhanging the east side over a timber deck, deep concrete beams running out east over the bedroom patios, carrying wires for climbers), the roof view (gravel roof inside a concrete upstand, U-shaped planter beams) and the pool view (the pool raised, stone-clad on a concrete base). The slope is drawn as a bank of the place's soil rising east to a level terrace; the living slab rises toward the east as the article describes (drawn as a wedge, since a thin tilted slab cannot be drawn). Which end is the living room, the second house's length (~14 m against ~24 m) and the pool sitting on the east terrace between them are read from the distant views and guessed; plans were not shown. The stone is the renderer's stone, lighter and warmer than the real grey-brown rubble. Scrub is shown as a few low trees and bushes; the pirca (dry-stone wall) below the houses is a straight run."
}